/// `merge`: land a reviewed worker's branch in one command.
pub(super) const TEXT: &str = "`mini-swe-mcp merge <worker_id>` lands a finished worker's branch on the base branch recorded for it: it trial-merges, runs the verify gate on the merge result in a throwaway worktree, merges with --no-ff and then deletes the branch, its history file and its worktree leftovers. It refuses - changing nothing - while the worker still runs, while the repository has another branch checked out, while a file the merge would touch has uncommitted changes (untracked and unrelated files are left alone), or while the branch conflicts, in which case it names the conflicting files and the steer that sends them back. The gate is skipped when the branch already contains the base tip and the worker's last verify passed. `--no-delete` keeps the branch. Merging a consolidator re-proves its round first: every worker of the round it was dispatched for -- not only the ones it recorded as integrated, so a member it listed as not ready and left out counts too -- must be missing (nothing left to land) or already integrated, by history (its tip reachable from the consolidator branch) or by content (merging it into that branch changes nothing, which is what a squash or cherry-pick leaves behind). A worker revised after the integration, or one the round never took, therefore REFUSES the merge, naming each worker and how many of its commits are unintegrated. Absorbed members and discarded ones (no branch) are expected and never refuse. Either steer the consolidator to integrate them, or discard the worker if its extra commits should not land; `merge <id> --force` lands the round anyway, and leaves those workers unretired so nothing is lost. `merge --approved` refuses on the same proof and has no `--force` of its own: land that one worker on its own. `merge --approved [--group <group>]` lands every approved worker of a group with ONE gate on the combined result: it composes the branches in approval order, skips a conflicting one and reports it with the steer that sends it back, runs the shared verify command once, and only then merges each branch with --no-ff and cleans up. It never pushes.";
